  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nLow clouds are currently spreading southwest across the region early \nthis morning and will linger over central/eastern Kansas \nsimilar to yesterday with a gradual erosion by early afternoon.\nThere is some potential for fog development early this morning \nalong the peripheral of the low cloud deck which looks to be \nmore transient. This transient behavior could limit dense fog \ndevelopment or keep it more brief. The quiet weather pattern \nwill continue for central/eastern Kansas today through Wednesday\nwith surface high pressure continuing to dominate over the \nregion. Temperatures will remain near seasonal averages for late\nSeptember with light east/northeast winds. \n\nCurrent satellite water vapor imagery shows upper troughing over the \nwestern states with an upper wave over the eastern Pacific \napproaching southern California. Also of note is another short wave \nnear the Baja region moving north. Still seeing a consistent signal \nfrom the models with strengthening low level jet transporting deeper \nmoisture from the Texas panhandle northward into western Kansas \nWednesday and Thursday. This is in response to the approaching upper \nwaves moving into the southwestern states. Numerous showers/storms \nare expected to develop over western Kansas Wed-Thu as lead \nshort wave near the Baja migrates northward into Colorado. The \nbulk of this activity will remain west of central Kansas until \nThursday when the axis of moisture/lift shifts slowly eastward. \nPeriodic on/off showers and storms will mainly affect \ncentral/south central Kansas Thursday through Friday night. The \ncombination of poor mid-level lapse rates and limited \ninstability keeps strong/severe thunderstorm potential very low \nthis period. Models show second upper level wave ejecting east \nfrom the Rockies into Nebraska/Kansas on Saturday with another \nround of showers/storms. Saturday could experience a slight \nuptick for instability levels but signal for severe storm \npotential still looks low. Longer range models/GEFS ensembles \nare suggesting a more pronounced upper wave racing east across \nthe Rockies and possibly ejecting out into the central plains \nfor early next week.",
